From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 16:25:56 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 34/46] [VideoPlayer] Stop forcing slice threading for
 multiview

Frame threading used to be unusable for multiview: the dependent view of an
access unit predicts from the base view of that same access unit, so ffmpeg
could not overlap the two and the threads only added synchronisation. Slice
threading alone was the faster of the two by a wide margin, and the decoder
asked for it explicitly.

ffmpeg now supports frame threading for multiview and deblocks postponed
slices in parallel, which reverses the comparison. Measured on a four core
Pi 4 against a six slice H.264 MVC stream, 1000 frames: 16.1s with slice
threading, 13.9s with frame threading, where the same clip on the previous
ffmpeg took 19.3s and 33.6s. Core use over the same runs rose from 2.3 to
3.3 of 4.

Drop the override and let the decoder choose, which gives it both thread
types. The thread count goes back to the core count as well: with frame
threading available, four and six threads are within noise of each other,
so the slice count no longer needs matching.

(cherry picked from commit fa09615103244101bd3598e936ed754c456ac58f)
